Three C details drove the translation, and each is a place a naive port would silently diverge:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mprintf("%02x\n", charHex)[0m — the [38;5;10mchar[0m argument is promoted to [38;5;10mint[0m, then [38;5;10m%x[0m reinterprets those bits as [38;5;10munsigned int[0m. [38;5;10m02[0m is a [3mminimum[23m width, so the negative result in [38;5;10mbad()[0m prints eight hex digits ([38;5;10mfffffffe[0m), not two. Modeled as [38;5;10mchar_hex as i32 as u32[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mchar result = data * 2[0m in [38;5;10mbad()[0m — [38;5;10m127 * 2[0m is computed in [38;5;10mint[0m (254) and truncated back into a signed [38;5;10mchar[0m, wrapping to [38;5;10m-2[0m. This is the CWE-190 overflow under test, so I reproduced it with an explicit [38;5;10m(data as i32 * 2) as i8[0m rather than letting Rust's debug overflow checks or a widened type "fix" it.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mscanf("%d", &x)[0m — on a matching failure or EOF, [38;5;10mx[0m keeps its initial [38;5;10m0[0m and the program takes the [38;5;10mbad()[0m branch. My [38;5;10mscanf_int[0m returns [38;5;10mNone[0m in those cases and the caller leaves [38;5;10mx[0m alone. It skips leading whitespace across newlines, accepts an optional sign, requires at least one digit, and mirrors glibc's [38;5;10mstrtol[0m-then-store-to-[38;5;10mint[0m path by saturating at [38;5;10mi64[0m bounds and keeping the low 32 bits. That last part is what makes [38;5;10m4294967296[0m (truncates to 0 → [38;5;10mbad()[0m) and [38;5;10m9223372036854775807[0m (→ [38;5;10m-1[0m → [38;5;10mgood()[0m) agree with the C.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two notes on fidelity choices worth your review. The dead store [38;5;10mdata = ' '[0m in [38;5;10mgoodB2G()[0m is retained behind an [38;5;10m#[allow(unused_assignments)][0m, since removing it would be a cleanup the instructions rule out. And [38;5;10mprintLine[0m's NULL guard is unrepresentable in safe Rust — a [38;5;10m&str[0m cannot be null, so the guard is always taken; I kept the function boundary but the branch is gone. That is the one construct I could not translate structurally, and it has no effect on output.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One limit on the verification: [38;5;10mscanf[0m overflow past [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m is undefined behavior in C, so my [38;5;10mi64[0m-saturating emulation matches [3mthis[23m glibc build on x86-64 Linux rather than the C standard. Both binaries agreed on every overflow case I tried, but a different libc could legitimately differ there. The signed-[38;5;10mchar[0m assumption ([38;5;10mCHAR_MAX == 127[0m) is likewise platform-specific and would need revisiting on a target where plain [38;5;10mchar[0m is unsigned.[0m[0m
